          'The most common failure at this stage is producing a slogan instead of a claim. "A stronger Europe" is a slogan. "The proposed regulation will cut emissions by 40% while protecting 200,000 jobs in the automotive sector" is a claim. The slogan has no content that can be supported or attacked. The claim does — which means it can be proved, challenged, and defended. In public affairs campaigning, your audiences are often sophisticated: MEPs, commission officials, industry associations, journalists who cover your sector. They will not be moved by slogans.',
          'Your central claim should meet three tests. First, the uniqueness test: can your opponents say the same thing? If they can, the claim is not differentiating and you need to revise it. Second, the provability test: can you support this claim with evidence in the next thirty seconds of conversation? If not, the claim is too abstract or too broad. Third, the translation test: does the claim survive translation into the other languages of your campaign without losing its core meaning? Idioms, metaphors, and culturally specific references fail this test constantly.',
          'Developing the central claim typically takes longer than teams expect and almost always requires qualitative research — focus groups, interviews with target audience members, expert input — before it can be finalised. Do not shortcut this. The central claim is the foundation. If it is wrong, everything built on top of it is built on sand.',
