          'Research across political psychology consistently shows that voters and stakeholders process political information through an emotional filter before they engage with its content. This is not a flaw in human cognition — it is the mechanism by which values translate into judgements. Your campaign\'s emotional rationale must name the values that your central claim serves, and it must name them in terms that your audiences recognise as their own.',
          'In an EU context, this is complicated by the genuine variation in values across member states. Research shows meaningful differences between how northern and southern European publics weight, for example, economic security versus economic freedom, national sovereignty versus European solidarity, or environmental protection versus industrial competitiveness. A campaign that assumes a uniform values landscape across the EU will produce an emotional rationale that resonates deeply in one country and lands badly in another.',
          'The solution is not to abandon the values layer, but to identify the values that have genuinely cross-EU resonance for your specific issue, and to express them at the right level of abstraction. "Fairness" tends to travel well. "Hard-working families" is a culturally specific Anglo-American formulation that does not translate. "The future our children will inherit" has proven cross-cultural resonance on environmental issues. The work of developing the emotional rationale involves researching which values frames your specific audiences hold in common, and then expressing them in language that has not been flattened by overuse.',
