          'Proof points should be: specific rather than vague, evidenced rather than asserted, and varied in type. A campaign whose proof points are all statistical will fail to reach audiences who respond more to testimony and narrative. A campaign whose proof points are all case studies will be picked apart by audiences who demand quantitative evidence. A well-built proof points layer includes at least one piece of hard data, at least one human-scale example or testimony, and at least one expert or institutional endorsement. This variety means you have material that can be adapted to different channels and different audience segments without exhausting any single type of evidence.',
          'The contrast layer is where campaigns most often go wrong. Two failure modes dominate. The first is the invented contrast: claiming a difference that is not genuinely there, or exaggerating a difference to the point where it becomes dishonest. This is dangerous not just ethically but tactically — sophisticated opponents and journalists will expose it, and a campaign that has been caught overstating its contrast is significantly weaker than one that never made the claim. The second failure mode is the absent contrast: producing proof points and then failing to draw any conclusion about what makes this campaign or position different. Without contrast, proof points are a list of facts in search of a campaign.',
          'Contrast should be earned, not invented. It should be based on a genuine, evidenced difference — in track record, in policy position, in approach, in the values the campaign represents. In EU public affairs, contrast is often most powerful when it is drawn against a process or a status quo rather than against a named opponent: "The current approach will cost x; our proposal will achieve y at a cost of z" is often more persuasive to commission officials than a contrast that names a rival industry coalition.',
