          'The reverse-engineering trap is the single most common failure mode in EU public affairs messaging. A campaign produces three months of content, then — usually prompted by poor poll numbers or a series of bad press interactions — commissions a messaging review. The review team is handed a pile of existing content and asked to build a message house from it. The result is a house that describes what the campaign has already said rather than what it should say. This is backwards. The house must precede the content. If you find yourself building a message house from your existing outputs, you are writing a retrospective, not a strategy.',
          'The committee claim is the second most common failure. Message houses that are developed by large, cross-functional teams without a clear decision-making process tend to produce central claims by consensus — and consensus produces claims that no one objects to because they mean nothing. "We believe in a sustainable, competitive, and fair Europe" is a committee claim. It cannot be supported, attacked, or differentiated, because its terms are so broad that everyone already agrees with it. Build the house in a small room with a clear decision-maker, test it widely, and then fix it in a small room again.',
