    keyFramework: {
      name: 'The four-function war room model',
      description: 'A model for dividing war room responsibilities into four distinct functions, each with a named lead and clear outputs.',
      rows: [
        ['Monitoring', 'Detect and assess', 'Real-time tracking of the issue across media, social, and stakeholder channels'],
        ['Decision-making', 'Respond or hold', 'Named authority decides whether to respond, in what form, and on what timeline'],
        ['Content production', 'Draft and review', 'Copy, creative, and approval in compressed timeframes with compliance checks'],
        ['Publishing', 'Deploy and track', 'Multi-channel distribution with real-time performance monitoring'],
      ],
    },
    previewSection: {
      title: 'The first decision: whether to respond',
      paragraphs: [
        'The most important decision in any rapid response situation is not what to say, but whether to say anything at all. This is counterintuitive for communications teams trained to believe that silence is always dangerous. The reality is more nuanced: responding to an attack amplifies it. If an opponent\'s attack is poorly targeted, has low reach, or is factually so weak that it will collapse under scrutiny without your help, engaging with it lends it credibility it would not otherwise have. The question "should we respond?" must always precede the question "what should we say?"',
        'The response decision should be made against a pre-agreed framework that weighs four factors: the reach of the attack (how many people have seen it, in what outlets or platforms, with what level of credibility?), the damage potential (if the attack is not countered, what is the realistic worst-case impact on the campaign?), the counter-availability (do we have a clear, credible, fast-deployable counter-argument?), and the amplification risk (would responding cause more people to see the attack than have seen it already?). A framework agreed before the crisis means the decision can be made in minutes by a named authority, rather than requiring a committee discussion that takes hours the campaign does not have.',
        'When the decision is to respond, the response form is the next question. Not every response requires a public statement. Some attacks are best handled through targeted outreach to journalists who are considering covering the story. Some require a direct social media counter. Some require a formal legal letter. Some require activating third-party validators rather than responding directly. The war room lead must match the response form to the nature of the attack, not default to the response format that feels most familiar.',
      ],
    },
    fullSections: [
      {
        title: 'Staffing and roles: the minimum viable war room',
        paragraphs: [
          'A war room does not require a large team. The minimum viable war room for a medium-sized campaign requires five roles: the war room lead, who holds decision-making authority and is the named approver for all outgoing content; the monitor, who tracks the attack across all relevant channels in real time and feeds intelligence to the lead; the drafter, who produces rapid content to brief specifications and can work in compressed timeframes; the compliance reviewer, who screens all outgoing content against the eight-check framework before publication, even in an accelerated timeline; and the publisher, who manages distribution across channels and monitors the response\'s performance in real time.',
          'In smaller campaigns or in the early hours of a fast-moving situation, one person may hold two of these roles — but the war room lead and the compliance reviewer should never be the same person. The compliance function exists precisely to act as a check on the war room lead\'s judgements under pressure. Combining the roles removes the check at the moment it is most needed.',
          'Pre-war room preparation includes: establishing and testing the monitoring feeds that the monitor will use; agreeing and documenting the response decision framework; pre-drafting response templates for the three most likely attack scenarios; confirming that all five role-holders know they are in the war room rotation and have the necessary access to publishing platforms; and running a tabletop exercise that takes the team through at least one simulated attack from detection to resolution. Teams that have run the exercise are measurably faster and more accurate in real crisis conditions than teams that have not.',
        ],
      },
    ],
