    slug: 'how-to-prepare-spokesperson-hostile-interview',
    title: 'How to Prepare a Spokesperson for a Hostile Interview',
    subheadline: 'A step-by-step preparation framework for getting a spokesperson ready for adversarial print, radio, and television interviews — from the brief through the debrief.',
    type: 'Playbook',
    pillarSlug: 'training-operations-management',
    branchSlug: 'training',
    topicSlug: 'media-interview-preparation',
    jurisdiction: 'EU-wide',
    countries: [],
    difficulty: 'Practitioner',
    readingTime: 9,
    lastUpdated: '2026-05-01',
    authorId: 'jonas-berg',
    locked: true,
    whatItCovers: 'The full preparation process for a hostile media interview, including how to write the brief, how to run the practice session, how to train for the most likely difficult questions, and how to debrief effectively.',
    whoItIsFor: 'Communications managers, media trainers, and campaign staff responsible for preparing spokespeople for broadcast and print interviews.',
    whenToUseIt: 'Before any media interview where the spokesperson is likely to face adversarial or challenging questioning. The framework scales from a thirty-minute radio interview to a major television appearance.',
    keyTakeaway: 'Preparation is not about memorising answers. It is about building the spokesperson\'s confidence and reflexes so they can deliver message under pressure without it feeling mechanical. Rote answers collapse under follow-up; internalized messages hold.',
    inBrief: [
      'The preparation brief should be shorter than the spokesperson wants and more demanding than they expect.',
      'The single most important preparation exercise is a realistic simulated interview — not a Q&A session.',
      'The three most likely difficult questions must be identified and practised before the actual interview.',
      'Message discipline under follow-up is harder than delivering the initial answer — practice the second and third follow-up.',
      'The debrief is as important as the preparation — what the spokesperson learned from the simulation changes their performance in the real interview.',
    ],
    keyFramework: {
      name: 'The four-stage interview preparation process',
      description: 'A structured preparation framework that takes a spokesperson from initial brief to post-simulation debrief before a hostile media interview.',
      rows: [
        ['Stage 1: Brief', 'Context and objectives', 'One page: interviewer profile, outlet, known lines of attack, and the three messages to land'],
        ['Stage 2: Q&A preparation', 'Map the difficult terrain', 'Identify the hardest questions and draft complete answers — not bullet points'],
        ['Stage 3: Simulated interview', 'Practise under pressure', 'A realistic hostile simulation with a skilled questioner — not a friendly run-through'],
        ['Stage 4: Debrief', 'Fix and reinforce', 'A structured review of what held and what collapsed, with targeted repetition of weak points'],
      ],
    },
    previewSection: {
      title: 'The brief: shorter, harder, and more focused than you think',
      paragraphs: [
        'The preparation brief is the foundation of the whole process. In most campaign environments, briefs are too long, too broad, and too reassuring. A well-written preparation brief for a hostile interview is a single page. It covers: a one-paragraph profile of the interviewer (their known style, their past treatment of similar subjects, their likely agenda); a sentence on the outlet and its audience; a list of the three most likely challenging lines of questioning; the three messages the spokesperson must land by the end of the interview; and the single most dangerous thing the spokesperson could say.',
        'The brief is not a fact pack. Fact packs create the illusion of preparation while encouraging the spokesperson to try to remember too much. In a hostile interview, a spokesperson who is trying to recall facts is not present in the conversation — they are rummaging in their memory. The brief\'s job is to focus attention on the few things that matter most, and to make the difficult questions visible rather than surprising.',
        'The most useful element of the brief is the "single most dangerous thing" — the specific statement or admission that, if made, would create the most significant damage for the campaign. Identifying this forces the preparation team to think adversarially rather than defensively, and it gives the spokesperson a concrete internal warning signal during the interview itself. When they feel the conversation moving toward that territory, they know to redirect.',
      ],
    },
    fullSections: [
      {
        title: 'Running the simulated interview: the only preparation that works',
        paragraphs: [
          'Question-and-answer sessions are not simulated interviews. A spokesperson who has answered questions in a comfortable briefing room, with time to think between questions, pausing to consult notes, is not prepared for a broadcast interview that moves at pace, with a hostile interviewer interrupting, reformulating questions, and returning to the same ground from different angles. The simulation must replicate the conditions of the real interview, including pace, discomfort, and the feeling of not knowing what comes next.',
          'The person conducting the simulation should be the most adversarial questioner available — ideally someone who has genuine broadcast journalism experience or who has been specifically trained for this role. The simulation should run without breaks for as long as the real interview will last. The spokesperson should not be given questions in advance. Interruptions should be built in. The most difficult question — the one the team identified in the brief — should come last, after the spokesperson is tired and has used up their best prepared answers.',
          'The simulation should be recorded. Not for the spokesperson to watch during the session — that is distracting and interrupts the flow — but for review in the debrief. The combination of the spokesperson\'s subjective sense of how the simulation went and the objective record of what actually happened is the most powerful learning tool available. Spokespeople are frequently surprised by the gap between the two: they felt confident about an answer that, on review, was evasive and unconvincing; they felt they had stumbled on a question that, on review, showed real strength.',
        ],
      },
    ],
    complianceBox: undefined,
    reviewer: undefined,
