          'Once the objective is established, the planning question shifts to who must change their behaviour for the objective to be achieved. The target is distinct from the audience. The target is the person or institution whose behaviour change is the direct mechanism for achieving the objective. The audience includes everyone the campaign communicates with — a larger and more varied group. Conflating the two is a common planning error that leads to diffuse, unfocused campaigns.',
          'In EU public affairs, there is a persistent temptation to define the target as "the public" or "EU citizens." This is almost never the correct answer. EU policy is made by a relatively small number of officials, legislators, and institutional actors. Changing their behaviour is the mechanism for changing policy. The public may feature in the theory of change — public pressure may be part of the causal mechanism — but it is rarely the direct target of the core campaign.',
          'Precision in target definition pays dividends throughout the plan. Once you know that your target is the twelve MEPs on the Committee on Industry, Research and Energy who are currently undecided on the amendment you are trying to insert, the questions of message, channel, and resource allocation become dramatically more tractable. What do these twelve individuals read? Who do they trust? What political pressures are they subject to? These are specific, answerable questions. "The public" is not a target — it is a planning evasion.',
          'For campaigns that genuinely have a public persuasion objective, the same principle of precision applies. "Young people" is not a sufficient target definition. "Young urban professionals in Germany and Poland aged 25–34 who identify as politically engaged but have not yet formed strong opinions on the energy transition" is a target definition that can inform message testing, channel selection, and creative development. The more specific the target definition, the more useful it is as a planning tool.',
