          'In EU advocacy, theories of change typically involve some combination of direct persuasion, coalition-building, public pressure, and technical evidence provision. The question for any specific campaign is which mechanisms are primary, in what sequence they operate, and what assumptions each one depends on. Surfacing the assumptions forces the planning team to confront weaknesses in the theory before they show up as failures in the campaign.',
          'With objective, target, and theory of change established, the message question becomes tractable. The message is not what the team wants to say. It is what the target must believe in order to behave differently. This reframing is critical. A campaign whose message is determined by what the team wants to communicate will produce outputs that feel good internally but do not move the target. A campaign whose message is determined by what the target needs to believe — and what currently prevents them from believing it — will produce communications calibrated to change behaviour.',
          'The message development process should begin with an honest assessment of where the target currently is. What do they currently believe? What are the main barriers — cognitive, political, or institutional — to them moving to the position the campaign needs? The answers determine the message. If the barrier is a belief that the policy would harm economic competitiveness, the message must address that concern with evidence that reframes the trade-off, not with a denial.',
