          'In multi-country EU campaigns, the channel mix will necessarily vary by country. The media landscape, the political culture, and the relative weight of digital versus traditional media differ significantly across member states. A campaign that assigns identical channel weights across France, Sweden, and Poland — where political communication operates very differently — will be inefficient in all three. The planning framework should specify a shared message and a differentiated channel mix, with national teams responsible for adapting within the shared strategic framework.',
          'Resource allocation is where strategy meets reality. The plan must map budget, staff time, and external capacity against the channel mix and timeline, and the mapping must be honest. A plan that requires twelve staff to produce in a team of four is not a plan — it is a wish list. The resource allocation step often forces a revision of earlier decisions: if available resources cannot support the theory of change at the required scale, either the theory must be simplified, the objective reduced in ambition, or more resources secured.',
          'The EU institutional calendar adds specific resource planning requirements that single-country campaigns do not face. The European Parliament\'s plenary calendar, committee schedules, and recess periods create windows and dead zones that must be built into the plan. Commission consultation periods and legislative timetables create deadlines largely outside the campaign\'s control. A plan that does not account for the EU institutional calendar will execute at the wrong moments and rest at the wrong moments — burning resources during recesses and arriving unprepared for key windows.',
